> > > You, of course, could put this in a pdf. And if you want your specific
> > > colors, then scale_color_manual() can give you this.
>
> > > The first thing to note is that overlaying as you were doing is the
> > > completely wrong paradigm with ggplot.
>
> > > Looking at m.myDf you'll see it actually is looking a bit akward with
> > > "values" and "value". What's going on here is that myDf is melted against
> > > the variable "values": melt moves each param variable, while keeping its
> > > original "values" column lined up,  into the "value" column (the melt
> > > default variable name for the values) while also creating a "variable"
> > > column which describes which param it is (as a factor variable). All
> > three
> > > params treated this way are then stacked row-wise, within these 3
> > columns.
>
> > > Because your only computing the density on 'values' where 'value' of
> > param
> > > is true, you can simply throw out wherever value==FALSE (ie keep
> > > which(value)). Then your data frame is already for ggplot.
>
> > > A melted data frame is exactly what ggplot eats up. Learning to construct
> > > data frames and use melt is a huge part of using ggplot and worth the
> > > initial head scratching. I remember the first time I tried it. hehe. A
> > rule
> > > of thumb, if it seems like too much code, it probably is.
>
> > > ~James
